---
import Layout from "../../../layouts/PageLayout.astro";

// Version MM-PRIVACY-v1.3, in force from 2026-10-27. Every statement about the app is
// backed by the ModelMeter source (privacy_points in modelmeter-ui/src/viewmodel.rs,
// the Android manifest and build.gradle.kts). Change this page in the same release
// that changes what the app does.
const metadata = {
  title: "ModelMeter Privacy Notice - Aunova",
  description:
    "How the ModelMeter Android app handles data: the device check stays on your phone, the free version shows Google ads, and the remove-ads purchase goes through Google Play.",
};
---

<Layout title={metadata.title} description={metadata.description}>
  <section class="legal-page">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="legal-header">
        <h1 class="legal-title">ModelMeter Privacy Notice</h1>
        <p class="legal-updated">Last updated: October 27, 2026</p>
      </div>

      <div class="legal-content">
        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>About this notice</h2>
          <p>
            This notice covers one thing: the <strong>ModelMeter</strong> app for Android
            (package <code>com.aunova.modelmeter</code>). ModelMeter checks which AI models your
            phone can run. This notice explains what happens to data when you use it. It covers
            both Aunova companies involved in the app.
          </p>
          <p>
            It does not cover the rest of aunova.net, which has its own
            <a href="/en/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
          </p>
          <p>The short version:</p>
          <ul class="legal-list">
            <li>The device check runs entirely on your phone. Nothing it works out is sent anywhere.</li>
            <li>We have no account, no sign-in, no analytics and no crash reporting of our own.</li>
            <li>
              The free version shows a Google ad only if you agree. If you say no, no ad is
              requested. If you say yes, Google's ad software collects data for the ad, and this
              notice says what.
            </li>
            <li>
              Neither Aunova company receives anything about you from the app. Aunova Technologies
              Ltd sees only Google's overall ad reports, such as how many ads were shown, which do
              not identify you.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Who is responsible, and how to reach us</h2>
          <p>Two Aunova companies are involved in ModelMeter.</p>
          <p>
            <strong>Aunova OÜ</strong>, Ahtri tn 12, 15551 Tallinn, Harju maakond, Estonia
            (registry code 16837438, VAT: EE102670613). Aunova OÜ makes ModelMeter and publishes it
            on Google Play. It decides how Google's ad software is built into the app and when the
            app asks for consent. It is also responsible for the purchase, the order records and the
            emails you send us.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Aunova Technologies Ltd</strong>, GA-00-SZ-L1-RT-164, Level 1, Gate Avenue -
            South Zone, Dubai International Financial Centre, Dubai, United Arab Emirates (DIFC
            licence CL13718). The ads in the free version run under Aunova Technologies Ltd's Google
            AdMob account. It sets up Google's consent form and receives the ad revenue.
          </p>
          <p>
            Both companies are responsible for the ads. You can contact either of them about the
            ads, and exercise your rights against either of them.
          </p>
          <p>
            You can reach both companies by email at
            <a href="mailto:chris@aunova.net">chris@aunova.net</a>, or by post at either address
            above.
          </p>
          <p>
            Neither company has appointed a Data Protection Officer. Data protection is handled
            internally, and the email address above reaches the person responsible for it at both
            companies.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>The device check stays on your phone</h2>
          <p>
            ModelMeter reads your phone's memory, free storage and hardware details, and compares
            them with a list of AI models. That list is built into the app.
          </p>
          <ul class="legal-list">
            <li>The check runs entirely on the phone. It works offline.</li>
            <li>What ModelMeter works out about your device is never sent anywhere, to us or to anyone else.</li>
            <li>
              ModelMeter does not save any of it to the phone's storage. The only thing saved on the
              phone is your answer about ads, explained under <strong>Consent for ads</strong>.
            </li>
            <li>There is no account and no sign-in.</li>
            <li>There is no analytics and no crash reporting of our own.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Permissions</h2>
          <p>
            ModelMeter never asks you to grant a permission. It needs none of the ones Android
            prompts for: no location, no contacts, no files, no phone state.
          </p>
          <p>
            The app does declare two permissions that Android grants without asking. Both are there
            for Google's ad software, not for anything the check does:
          </p>
          <ul class="legal-list">
            <li><strong>Internet</strong>, to fetch an ad and the consent form.</li>
            <li><strong>Advertising ID</strong>, because Google's ad software reads your phone's advertising ID.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Ads in the free version</h2>
          <p>
            The free version can show one banner ad, on the Models screen only, and only if you have
            agreed (see <strong>Consent for ads</strong>). The ad is the one part of the app that
            uses the network on its own. It comes from Google AdMob, through the Google Mobile Ads
            SDK, and runs under Aunova Technologies Ltd's AdMob account. If you have not agreed, no
            ad is requested.
          </p>
          <p>
            Once you have agreed, Google states that its Mobile Ads SDK collects and shares the
            following data automatically, for advertising, analytics and fraud prevention:
          </p>
          <ul class="legal-list">
            <li>
              <strong>IP address</strong>, which may be used to estimate the general location of
              your device.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Product interactions</strong>, such as app launches, taps and video views.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Diagnostic information</strong> about how the app and the SDK perform, such
              as app launch time, hang rate and energy usage.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Device and account identifiers</strong>: the Android advertising ID, the app
              set ID and, where applicable, other identifiers related to accounts signed in on the
              device.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Google states that this data is encrypted in transit. The source is Google's own
            <a href="https://developers.google.com/admob/android/privacy/play-data-disclosure">data
            disclosure for the Mobile Ads SDK</a>, which Google keeps up to date.
          </p>
          <p>
            Nothing ModelMeter works out about your device is part of the ad request. We do not pass
            Google any data of our own.
          </p>
          <p>
            Google uses the data it collects through its SDK under its own rules, and for AdMob it
            acts as an independent controller. Google may process this data outside your country,
            including in the United States. See
            <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google's privacy policy</a> and
            <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites">how Google uses
            information from sites or apps that use its services</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Consent for ads</h2>
          <p>
            Before the ad software requests anything, ModelMeter shows you Google's consent form, from
            Google's User Messaging Platform. Every user sees it, wherever you are. It includes a
            <strong>Do not consent</strong> option. No ad is requested unless you agree.
          </p>
          <p>If you say no, you see no ad and the app works exactly the same.</p>
          <p>
            <strong>What is saved on your phone.</strong> Google's consent tool saves your answer in
            ModelMeter's own storage on your phone, in the standard format of the IAB Transparency
            and Consent Framework. It does this so that you are not asked every time you open the
            app, and so that Google's ad software can read what you allowed it to do. Your answer is
            not sent to us. Apart from it, ModelMeter saves nothing about you on the phone.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Changing your mind.</strong> The About screen has a
            <strong>Privacy options</strong> button. It reopens Google's consent form, so you can
            change your answer or withdraw consent at any time. If you withdraw consent, you stop
            seeing the ad: from then on, no ad is requested. Withdrawing does not affect what
            happened before.
          </p>
          <p>You can also:</p>
          <ul class="legal-list">
            <li>
              <strong>Reset or delete your advertising ID</strong> with the ad ID controls in your
              phone's Android settings. This works for every app on the phone, not just ModelMeter.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Clear ModelMeter's storage</strong> in Android settings, under Apps, then
              ModelMeter. That deletes your saved answer, and you are asked again the next time the
              app would request an ad.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Buy the remove-ads option</strong>, described below. When Google Play
              confirms, as the app opens, that the purchase is on your Google account, ModelMeter
              neither shows the consent form nor starts the ad software. If Google Play has not
              answered within about three seconds of the app opening, ModelMeter treats that session
              as the free version. On such a start a buyer can still see the consent form and,
              after agreeing, the ad.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>The remove-ads purchase</h2>
          <p>
            A one-time in-app purchase removes the ad. Nothing else changes: the check and the
            model list are the same either way. "Restore purchases" on the About screen brings the
            purchase to another device signed in to the same Google account.
          </p>
          <p>
            The purchase runs through Google Play Billing. Google handles the payment. We never see
            your card or any other payment details.
          </p>
          <p>
            The app reads from Google Play only what it needs to remove the ad: whether this
            Google account owns the purchase, and the purchase token Google uses to identify it.
            The app uses that token only to confirm the purchase to Google Play. It is not sent
            to us.
          </p>
          <p>
            Aunova OÜ, as the Google Play developer, can see an order record for each sale in Google
            Play Console. Google decides what it contains. It includes an order number, the date,
            the product, the price and tax, and the buyer's country, and in some cases the region
            and postcode. Aunova OÜ uses it only for its accounts.
          </p>
          <p>
            See the <a href="https://play.google.com/about/play-terms/">Google Play Terms of
            Service</a> and <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google's privacy
            policy</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Links that leave the app</h2>
          <p>
            Some things in ModelMeter open a web address. Tapping one hands that address to your
            browser and leaves ModelMeter. From there, the site you visit and its own privacy
            policy apply. Nothing about your device or your check goes with the link.
          </p>
          <ul class="legal-list">
            <li>
              <strong>A model's page</strong> opens on Hugging Face. See the
              <a href="https://huggingface.co/privacy">Hugging Face privacy policy</a>.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Tideproof</strong>, Aunova's other product, opens at tideproof.org. The link
              is tagged so that tideproof.org can tell the visit came from ModelMeter. See the
              <a href="https://tideproof.org/privacy/">Tideproof privacy page</a>.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>The banner ad</strong>, in the free version, opens whatever the advertiser's
              link points to. That link is not ours. What Google's ad software sends with the tap is
              covered by Google's policies above, and the advertiser's site has its own.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Our lawful basis</h2>
          <ul class="legal-list">
            <li>
              <strong>The device check</strong> involves no processing of personal data by us. It
              runs on your phone and nothing leaves it.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Ads.</strong> Consent is the only basis, for every user. Aunova OÜ and Aunova
              Technologies Ltd rely on your consent for having the ad software store and read
              information on your phone, and collect your data and send it to Google: GDPR Article
              6(1)(a), and the consent rules for information stored on your device. For Aunova
              Technologies Ltd, this is also consent under Article 10(a) of the DIFC Data
              Protection Law 2020. No ad is requested without your consent, and you can withdraw it
              at any time (see <strong>Consent for ads</strong>). Google relies on its own lawful
              bases for what it does with the data afterwards; see Google's privacy policy.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>The purchase.</strong> Aunova OÜ handles the purchase status so that the ad
              is removed, because that is necessary to give you what you paid for: Article 6(1)(b).
              Keeping order records for its accounts is a legal obligation: Article 6(1)(c).
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>If you email us</strong>, the company you write to uses your message to answer
              it, on the basis of its legitimate interest in replying to you: Article 6(1)(f).
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>Neither company makes any decision about you automatically.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
          <p>Neither Aunova company receives any data about you from the app, so neither keeps any.</p>
          <ul class="legal-list">
            <li>
              <strong>Your answer about ads</strong> stays on your phone until you change it, clear
              ModelMeter's storage or uninstall the app.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Order records</strong> stay in Google Play Console. Aunova OÜ keeps them for
              as long as accounting law requires.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Emails you send us</strong> are kept until your question is settled, and then
              deleted.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            What Google keeps from its ad software and from Google Play is set by Google's
            policies, linked above.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Your rights</h2>
          <p>
            You have the following rights over any personal data we hold about you. Exercising any
            of them is free, and you never have to justify asking.
          </p>
          <ul class="legal-list">
            <li><strong>Access</strong> to the data and a copy of it. GDPR Article 15.</li>
            <li><strong>Rectification</strong> of inaccurate data. Article 16.</li>
            <li><strong>Erasure</strong> of your data. Article 17.</li>
            <li><strong>Restriction</strong> of what we do with it. Article 18.</li>
            <li><strong>Portability</strong> of the data you gave us. Article 20.</li>
            <li><strong>Objection</strong> to processing based on our legitimate interest. Article 21.</li>
            <li>
              <strong>Withdraw consent</strong> at any time, without affecting what was done before.
              Article 7(3). For ads, see <strong>Consent for ads</strong> above.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Complain</strong> to a supervisory authority, and seek a remedy in court. See
              below.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Because the app sends us nothing, we usually cannot link any data to you from ModelMeter
            alone. The data the ad software collects is held by Google. To exercise your rights over
            it, use Google's own tools, described in
            <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google's privacy policy</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            To ask us, email <a href="mailto:chris@aunova.net">chris@aunova.net</a> or write to
            either postal address above. For the ads, you can ask either company. You do not need to
            cite a law or fill in a form. We answer without undue delay and within one month of
            receiving your request. If a request is complex, or we receive many, we may extend that
            by up to two further months. If we do, we tell you within the first month, and why
            (GDPR Article 12(3)).
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Complaining to a supervisory authority</h2>
          <p>
            We would rather hear from you first, but you do not have to come to us at all. You can
            complain to the data protection authority of the country where you live, where you work,
            or where you think the problem happened, and you may seek a remedy in court.
          </p>
          <p>
            The authority for Aunova OÜ is the <strong>Estonian Data Protection
            Inspectorate</strong> (<em>Andmekaitse Inspektsioon</em>), Tatari 39, 10134 Tallinn,
            Estonia. Telephone +372 627 4135. Website <a href="https://www.aki.ee">www.aki.ee</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Aunova Technologies Ltd is also subject to the DIFC Data Protection Law 2020, and you
            can complain about it to the DIFC Commissioner of Data Protection, Dubai International
            Financial Centre, Dubai, United Arab Emirates. Website
            <a href="https://www.difc.com/business/registrars-and-commissioners/commissioner-of-data-protection">difc.com</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Children</h2>
          <p>ModelMeter is not directed at children.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Changes to this notice</h2>
          <p>
            We update this notice when what the app does changes, and the version and date change
            with it. If a new version of the app sends anything new off the phone, this notice
            changes first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Version and effective date</h2>
          <p>
            Version <strong>MM-PRIVACY-v1.3</strong>, in force from <strong>27 October 2026</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>This notice is pending review by qualified counsel. It is not legal advice.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .legal-page {
    padding: var(--space-3xl) 0 var(--space-5xl);
    min-height: calc(100vh - var(--header-height));
  }

  .legal-header {
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3xl);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-2xl);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .legal-title {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--color-primary), var(--color-secondary));
    background-size: 200% 200%;
    animation: gradient-shift 5s ease infinite;
  }

  .legal-updated {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .legal-content {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .legal-section {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2xl);
  }

  .legal-section h2 {
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-lg);
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  .legal-intro {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  .legal-section p {
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  .legal-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: var(--space-md) 0;
  }

  .legal-list li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: var(--space-xl);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  .legal-list li::before {
    content: "→";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    color: var(--color-primary);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .legal-list li strong {
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .legal-section a {
    color: var(--color-primary);
    text-decoration: underline;
    transition: opacity var(--transition-fast);
  }

  .legal-section a:hover {
    opacity: 0.8;
  }

  @keyframes gradient-shift {
    0%, 100% { background-position: 0% 50%; }
    50% { background-position: 100% 50%; }
  }

  /* Mobile styles */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .legal-page {
      padding: var(--space-2xl) 0 var(--space-3xl);
    }

    .legal-header {
      margin-bottom: var(--space-2xl);
      padding-bottom: var(--space-xl);
    }

    .legal-section {
      margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
    }

    .legal-section h2 {
      font-size: 1.5rem;
    }

    .legal-intro {
      font-size: 1rem;
    }
  }
</style>
